# Structure

# WBB

# Boris

The `Boris` structure is optimized for path access. It looks like this:

```
<namespace> <tag-name> <content> <attribute-namespace> <attribute-name> <value>
```

## namespace

Namespace is optional and it’s type is `refinement!`.

## tag-name

Tag name is converted to `word!`. It’s required value.

## content

Content is either `block!` when the tag has children or `string!` when not.
Content is required and always follows tag-name so tag-names can be used in
paths.

## attribute-namespace

Attribute-namespace follows the same rules as normal namespaces. They are
optional and their type is `refinement!`

## attribute-name

Attribute names are optional and their type is `issue!`.

## attribute value

Attribute value follows attribute-name and is always a `string!`.

# Old info


The current structure of converted data is:

```
<tag-name> <content> <attributes>
```

## tag-name

`tag-name` is converted to `word!` if possible or `path!` if it contains
namespace. If it’s not possible to convert to Red type, it’s kept as `string!`.
This behaviour can be controled with settings `xml/red-keys?`.
`tag-name` can be `none!` for content that’s not enclosed in tag.

```
<b>Hello<c>world</c></b>
```

will be represented as:

[
	b [
		none "Hello" none
		c "world" #()
	] #()
]

## content

`content` can be

- `block!` if the element has children
- `string!` if it has only textual content
- `none!` for some metadata

## attributes

`attributes` can be either `map!` or `none!`. `none!` is used for textual
content and some metadata. Attribute names are converted to `word!` if possible
or kept as strings. This can be controlled with the `xml/red-keys?` setting.
Unlike `tag-name`, attribute names with namespace cannot be converted to paths
as `map!` doesn’t support `path!` as keys.

# Problems

We want to convert tag and attribute names to Red types for easier access,
however, that’s not always possible. There are some edge cases, but they are
rare so we can safely ignore them (names can start with a colon `:`).
The bigger problem are namespaces. Conversion to `path!` is an obvious solution,
however, this gives us `path!` as keys for tag names, which is unusual in Red,
but possible. But for attribute names, it’s impossible to use `path!` as a key
in `map!`.

# Solutions

There are multiple solutions, each has it’s pros and cons. We can change
the type of `attributes` to `block!` - this way it would be possible `path!`
as an attribute name. This brings some minor problems: 

- Visually there would be no difference between content and attributes
- In some cases getting attribute value would be impossible with `select`

Consider this example:

```
<tag att1="att2" att2="smth">content</tag>
```

If we turn off conversion to Red types, this XML would be represented as:

```
[
	tag "content" [
		"att1" "att2"
		"att2" "smth"
	]
]
```

`select attributes "att2"` would return `"att2"` instead of `"smth"`
so a different mechanism must be used. This can be solved actually very easy
with `select/skip attributes att-name 2`. Also, using blocks, the reason why
keys should be kept as `string!` is almost non-existent.
